## Making File System Statics Testable

The best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's a battle-tested library that wraps the entire `System.IO` surface behind mockable interfaces.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs or Startup.cs:
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
[Test]
public void Load_ReturnsFileContent()
{
    var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
    {
        { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
    });

    var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

    Assert.AreEqual("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Load("/config.json"));
}

[Test]
public void Save_WritesContent()
{
    var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
    mockFs.Directory.CreateDirectory("/data");

    var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);
    manager.Save("/data/out.txt", "hello");

    Assert.AreEqual("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));
}
```

### Why this over a custom wrapper?

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| `System.IO.Abstractions` | Complete API surface, `MockFileSystem` included, community-maintained | Extra NuGet dependency |
| Custom `IFileService` | No dependencies | You only cover what you wrap; easy to miss edge cases |

**Recommendation:** Use `System.IO.Abstractions` unless you have a policy against third-party packages. It covers `File.*`, `Directory.*`, `Path.*`, and more — so you won't need to expand your wrapper later.
